:preface.Preface
:p.I have tried to compose this to be as turnkey as possible, but I
will have inevitably made mistakes somewhere. If you find any, please
email me at :hp3.wec@bam.moe:ehp3.!
:p.AI statement: no generative AI was used in the composition of this
work.
:p.This document was formatted on an IBM System/390 Multiprise 3000
mainframe running z/VM 4.4 with BookMaster 1.4.
:toc.
:figlist.
:tlist.
:body.
:h1.An Introduction to Packet Radio
:p.Packet radio is a very advanced world, and I assure you that this
guide only barely does the protocol family justice. Amateur packet
radio has a rich history of protocol development and asserting an
influence on later network protocol stacks, and played a pivotal
development in the popularization of the Internet Protocol with
hobbyists.
:h3.Original Packet Radio
:p.In the past, computer networks were not nearly as commonplace,
accessible, or affordable-to-construct as they are today. Before the
modern era, the ARPAnet was inaccessible to anyone that wasn't a
government employee, contractor, or university student in the US; while
that did exist, the world was dotted by numerable :hp1.corporate
networks:ehp1. that used strange protocols, unusual hardware, and were
of no interest to the casual hobbyist. Where the ARPAnet required the
blessing of the US government and enterprise networks required strange
mainframes, communications controllers the size of refrigerators, and
things like that, the amateur radio operators of the late 1970s and
early 1980s looked on to things their peers had developed :hp1.but
seemed so out-of-touch.:ehp1.
:p.An important milestone in the development of computing networking
was Professor Norman Abramson at the University of Hawaii's project:
:hp2.ALOHAnet.:ehp2. The Hawaiian Islands were separated by oceans, and
it was not affordable for a group of universities to lay cables into
the ocean to connect their sites; as such, Dr. Abramson sought a
wireless approach. He figured that since a single radio station could
cover a sizable portion of the Islands, couldn't the same approach work
for a wireless computer network?
:p.The development of ALOHAnet resulted in a 9600 baud UHF packet radio
system that had reasonably been perfected by 1975, and the Aloha
medium-multiple-access protocol had inspired many descendent
technologies.
:fn.An interesting offshoot of the Aloha protocol was, of all things,
:hp1.Ethernet:ehp1.; the designers called it :q.Aloha in a wire:eq. and
were not far-off -- both systems used the same CSMA medium access
control technique.
:efn.
:p.In the San Francisco Bay, DARPA worked with SRI International to
construct a network called PRNET; this was the aptly-named :hp2.Packet
Radio NETwork.:ehp2. These researchers worked from 1973 to 1976 to run
the ARPAnet NCP protocol (which preceded the current Internet Protocol,
but is :hp1.radically:ehp1. different) wirelessly. Rather than using a
more primitive FSK modem design like the ALOHAnet project did, PRNET
used direct-sequence-spread-spectrum modems that included a forward
error correction algorithm -- this resulted in 100 kbps and 400 kbps
radio channels filling the air. Most pivotally, the PRNET project
demonstrated the first :hp1.internetworking:ehp1. wherein the ARPAnet,
PRNET, and SATNET (a satellite communications network) were interlinked.
:fn.The site of the first internetworking was a GMC Value-Van that Don
Cone had filled with radio equipment and computer equipment (which was
a DEC PDP-11/03 LSI-11 minicomputer); on November 22, 1977, the
Internet was born.
:efn.
:h3.Ham Packet Radio
:p.Amateur packet radio started in Montreal, Quebec in May 1978: VE2PY
(Robert Rouleau), VE2BQS (Norm Pearl), and VE2EHP (Jacques Orsali)
acquired special authorization from the Canadian government (an
:q.Amateur Radio Digital Operator's Certificate:eq.) to transmit
ASCII-encoded data on the 1.25-meter amateur band with custom
equipment. On the other side of the continent, VE7APU (Doug Lockhart)
and his group, the Vancouver Area Digital Communications Group) began
to produce proper modems that were much more capable than the earlier
Montreal Amateur Radio Club work. These devices became known as
:hp3.Terminal Node Controllers.:ehp3.
:p.South of Canada in the United States, the FCC permitted ASCII
transmissions in 1980; shortly after that in December 1980, KA6M (Hank
Magnuski) had erected the first packet repeater on 2 meters. Since the
old NCP protocol had yet to be replaced, Hank scored a very valuable
victory: the :hp1.entirety:ehp1. of the :pk.44.0.0.0/8:epk. network was
allocated for amateur radio, and became known as AMPRnet.
:fn.In keeping with the ARPANET theme.
:efn.
:p.Two years later (in January 1983), NCP was replaced with TCP, and
RFC 790 listed it amongst several other networks.
:fn.Interestingly enough, the now-ubiquitous :pk.10.0.0.0/8:epk.
network was a direct mapping of the ARPANET address space -- the
Internet has had several major addressing scheme overhauls throughout
its history!
:efn.
:p.1982 was a pivotal year for packet radio. The Pacific Packet Radio
Society (California), the Tuscon Amateur Packet Radio Corporation
(TAPR, Arizona), and the Amateur Radio Research and Development
Corporation (AMRAD, Washington, D.C.) were hot on the trails of
developing consumer hardware for this new craze.
:p.All of these incompatible TNCs meant that, though everyone used Bell
202 modems, nobody could interlink with each other. Everyone claimed to
use a modified :q.Vancouver Protocol:eq. (based on the work of VADCG),
but nobody really was. In 1982, several meetings were held, consisting
of many of the big names in packet radio of the day. They realized they
needed a standard, and they eventually came up with the :q.Amateur
Radio Protocol.:eq.
:p.This protocol was based on the extremely popular X.25 protocol, and
was named AX.25 (more information on it will be presented after this
chapter). It was WB4JFI (Terry Fox), who was the head of the ARRL's
:hp1.Ad-Hoc Committee on Amateur Radio Digital Communication:ehp1. who
signed off on the standard after it had been decided; this occurred on
October 26, 1984.
:p.AX.25 was a massive improvement over the existing VADCG protocols.
For one, it could handle 128 connections at the same time -- on the eve
of AX.25's standardization, it was estimated that were no more than 200
hams using packet radio in the entirety of the US and Canada combined!
One of the early TNCs that was responsible for settling this issue was
produced by a company called :q.Bill Ashby and Sons:eq.; that company
built a simple board based off of the Vancouver protocol hardware and
software, and made it available. Richcraft Engineering, ran by W4UCH,
came up with an ingenious idea: use the CP/M microcomputer OS to
implement a software-based packet radio stack. This greatly simplified
part complexity, and removed the need to use actual modem parts (often
called :q.HDLC controllers:eq. because they were lifted straight from
wireline X.25 modem hardware). After that, GLB Electronics came onto
the scene with something that would become extremely familiar to anyone
that finds old TNCs at hamfests: they designed a simple and cheap
Z80-based microcomputer that drove the audio interface much in the same
way that the Richcraft board did; this was the PK-1, and it was the
first popular software-only TNC.
:p.The TAPR was a strange bunch, and consisted of not more than 20
amateurs. On a weekend, they all met in the University of Arizona's
Computer Science building, and pitched an interesting project idea:
:q.could we develop a cheap packet radio system that was capable of
transmitting microcomputer software via the airwaves?:eq.
:p.Interestingly, many of the TAPR engineers worked for Motorola, who
was just down the street; the efforts of that club were shown to the
world in 1982 at the First American Radio Relay League Computer
Networking Conference. Drawing on the successes of the past, they
launched a very good and high-quality TNC kit in July 1983; whereas the
earlier attempts were somewhat shoddy in their design and just as bad
in their manufacturing, the TAPR kits felt :hp1.almost
professional:ehp1. -- the quality of their kits readily rivalled
commercial radio vendors (as in, companies that made radio hardware for
the non-amateur :q.real world:eq. market). The first run of this TNC
was at 9:12 PM PST on June 25, 1982; blazing away on 146.55 MHz, KD2S
(Den Connors) and Lyle Johnson (WA7GXD) held the first contact using
Heath H-89 serial terminals.
:p.In 1984, the TAPR TNC-1 board took the world by storm. It wasn't for
lack of trying, though; the production of the boards was very troubled.
In December 1982, just as they were getting ready to launch the
aforementioned board, the TAPR guys were having nonstop solder problems
on their circuit board assembly line. This resulted in a mad dash to
fix the productions, but the group was successful -- the board took the
world by storm.
:p.As soon as these became commonplace, all manner of bulletin board
systems (BBSes) began to pop up. One of the first BBSes put up on the
East Coast was by AI2Q (Alex Mendelsohn), and was powered by a Xerox
820 desktop computer running CP/M. Digipeaters quickly cropped up,
bringing the footprint of these BBSes to hundreds of mile in a matter
of weeks. Since the packet radio stack ran on that Xerox 820, NW2X
(Howie Goldstein) had a bright idea during his time as a student at the
Florida Institute of Technology: use the surplus and obsolete Xerox 820
motherboards to greatly evolve the function of those TAPR TNC-1 boards!
:p.The TNC-2 was announced at the 1985 Dayton Hamvention, to amazing
acclaim. This was based on the same idea as the early Z80 soft-TNC
pioneers, but greatly upgraded after many had experimented with the
TNC-1 and Xerox 820 combination system. The TNC-2 contained a Z80, some
RAM, a ROM chip, and a modem interface; this was quickly licensed out
to several companies, who began manufacturing them all over the world.
By the early 90s, TNC-2 devices were ubiquitous. Packet radio had hit
it big, arguably much bigger than it is today.
:p.Now, it is up to you to breathe life back into this amazing
forgotten world in an age of an uncertain Internet future.
:h3.Bringing it Together
:p.Before proceeding, I recommend you familiarize yourself with some
key terms heard in packet radio:
:ul.
:li.Packet Radio: a collection of hardware and software that performs
network communications via radio
:li.TNC (Terminal Node Controller): a box or program that connects a
computer or terminal to a radio and performs the
modulation/demodulation tasks required to generate a baseband signal
containing data
:li.Node: a host on a packet radio network, usually capable of
receiving inbound connections
:li.BBS (Bulletin Board System): a special kind of node that stores
messages (and sometimes forwards them off to other nodes)
:eul.
:p.For 99% of packet radio installations, the TNC will connect to a
VHF/UHF FM radio through its :hp3.audio interface;:ehp3. it also likely
has the ability to :hp3.key the radio:ehp3. -- since accurate
transmit/receive timings are paramount for packet radio, :hp2.you
cannot use your radio's VOX control for bidirectional packet:ehp2. (and
you should not use it for unidirectional packet either, honestly)!
:lblbox.Audio filtering and high-speed packet
:p.For most people using a Baofeng HT or mobile radio, the highest
speed you will be able to attain is 2400 baud (with 1200 baud being the
most common, for things like APRS). This is not because packet radio
has not evolved (in fact, there are megabaud packet radio systems), but
because the radio applies an audio filter on both the microphone input
and the audio output.
:p.The audio input is filtered to cut off any audio below 300 Hz (this
is to prevent bass tones from tripping a repeater's PL tone decoder,
which may stop you from being able to transmit into the repeater for
the duration of your transmission) and above 4 KHz (to conserve FM
bandwidth); likewise, the audio output is filtered to remove anything
above 4 KHz and below 300 Hz (to prevent screws backing out due to
constant PL tone bass vibrations on the speaker, or so I'm told).
:p.As such, in order to go faster (such as up to 9600 baud, the
most-desired speed), one must do away with these filters. This is done
by :hp2.tapping the discriminator:ehp2. and :hp2.directly driving the
modulator.:ehp2. This can be done by examining the schematic for your
radio, but many VHF/UHF mobiles made through the late 1990s to today
contain a port at the back that has a 9600 baud packet output; note
that it is paramount that you check the manual to determine what the
output level is (too low of an output level results in poor reception)
and ensure you do not overdrive the modulator input (doing so could put
your transmissions out-of-spec).
:elblbox.
:fig width=column place=inline frame=none.
:artwork name=prlpf align=center.
:figcap.VHF/UHF LMR audio frequency range
:efig.
:h1.Surveying the Landscape: Protocols and Stacks
:p.When discussing packet radio, it helps to have an understanding of
the numerable protocols found on the air:
:ul.
:li.AX.25
:li.KISS
:li.NET/ROM
:li.ROSE
:li.TCP/IP
:li.APRS
:eul.
:p.Before we get carried away, it is a good idea to familiarize oneself
with these protocols:
:h3.AX.25 (and KISS)
:p.The primary network protocol of amateur packet radio is
:hp3.AX.25.:ehp3., and it is a :hp3.link layer protocol.:ehp3. This is
a protocol derived from the earlier (but once extremely popular)
:hp1.X.25:ehp1. protocol family, a stack of protocols that provided:
:i1.AX.25 Protocol
:ul.
:li.A relatively simple protocol family that could be implemented
anywhere from large mainframe computers to small boxes that attached to
terminals
:li.Packet-switched networking, wherein :q.virtual circuits:eq. would
be propped up like TCP sockets are today on the TCP/IP protocol family
:fn.Keep that :hp3.virtual circuit:ehp3. term in mind as you read this,
as it is critical to understanding AX.25
:efn.
:li.Congestion control, retransmission, and other such features for
reliable communications on network links of uncertain reliability
:eul.
:p.:hp1.If you are passingly-familiar with :hp3.TCP:ehp3., you may
realize that these X.25 features share significant overlap with TCP
features -- TCP was intended to emulate the X.25 PLP (packet layer
protocol) over IP.:ehp1.
:h5.Addressing Scheme
:p.AX.25 provides a very simple service: a virtual circuit, between two
callsigns (which includes the SSID). This means that AX.25 addresses
use ham radio callsigns, so :pk.WA4XYZ-6:epk. and :pk.N4ABC:epk. are
valid.
:p.The suffix after the dash is called the :hp3.SSID:ehp3., i.e. the
Service Set Identifier. This allows you to have 16 different AX.25
addresses with one callsign; by convention, you would not
write:pk.N4ABC-0:epk., but would instead write :pk.N4ABC:epk. without
the suffix.
:i2.SSID
:p.The fundamental unit of AX.25 communication is the :hp3.frame.:ehp3.
This contains the destination callsign, source callsign, protocol
number, and a control byte; the control byte selects the frame type
(shown below), and the protocol number identifies the packet contents
(also listed below).
:h5.Operating Modes
:p.AX.25 has two operating modes: disconnected and connected. In the
disconnected mode, the following frame types may be sent:
:ul.
:li.:pk.SABM:epk. (Set Asynchronous Balanced Mode), used to initiate a
connection
:li.:pk.DISC:epk. (Disconnect), to disconnect any stale connections
:li.:pk.XID:epk. (Exchange Identification), sometimes used to exchange
station protocol capabilities
:li.:pk.UA:epk. (Unnumbered Acknowledge), to acknowledge an SABM or
DISC frame
:li.:pk.UI:epk. (Unnumbered Information), a frame that contains a bit
of information blasted verbatim without being contained within a
virtual circuit
:eul.
:p.When station 1 sends an SABM frame to station 2, station 2 will
hopefully reply with a UA frame -- if this does happen, the two
stations will now be :q.connected:eq.. In this state, the following
frames can be sent:
:ul.
:li.:pk.I:epk. (Information), a frame that contains the virtual circuit
:q.payload:eq.; this is transmitted alongside a send and receive
sequence number (which is used to ensure the packets are arriving)
:li.:pk.RR:epk. (Receiver Ready), to inform the recipient of the packet
that it got it and to inform the transmitting station to proceed
sending more
:li.:pk.REJ:epk. (Reject), if one side receives an I-frame with an
invalid :pk.N(S):epk. value (i.e. send sequence number)
:li.:pk.RNR:epk. (Receiver Not Ready), if the receiving station is
unable to process packets for whatever reason (perhaps it is out of
computational resources)
:eul.
:h5.Packet Format
:p.As you may imagine, UI frames are often used for beacons or to carry
additional network protocols. Now, examine the following diagram of an
AX.25 frame:
:i2.Packet format
:fig width=column place=inline frame=none.
:artwork name=prax25 align=center.
:figcap.AX.25 frame structure
:efig.
:p.Note that the image here is good for either a numbered or unnumbered
frame (that is, connected-mode or unconnected-mode). It is a good idea
to break down the packet structure for additional clarity:
:ul.
:li.Address: the address field here is variable-length (as shown, 14 to
28 bytes) since this can accommodate a plain source/destination pair or
:hp3.up to two digipeaters:ehp3.. :hp1.A digipeater is a packet
repeater system, usually simplex; you can only have two digipeaters
along a path!:ehp1.
:i2.Digipeater
:li.Control: this conveys the field type, seen in the above list; it
may span two bytes -- this is necessary to accommodate the sequence
numbers (which run :hp3.0 to 7:ehp3. so 4 bits; :pk.N(R):epk.
and:pk.N(S):epk. fit nicely in one byte together)
:li.PID (Protocol ID): this specifies the protocol contained within the
packet. The current registered values are as follows:
:i2.PID/protocol numbers
:ul.
:li.0x01: ISO 8208 (X.25 Packet Layer Protocol)
:fn.See the section on ROSE for more information on this.
:efn.
:li.0x06: VJ-compressed TCP/IP
:fn.This is the same compression algorithm used for :q.IP Header
Compression:eq. on PPP networks.
:efn.
:li.0x07: VJ-compressed TCP/IP (for an uncompressed frame)
:li.0x08: Segmentation fragment
:li.0xC3: TEXNET Datagram Protocol
:li.0xC4: Link Quality Protocol (LQP)
:li.0xCA: AppleTalk
:li.0xCB: AARP (AppleTalk)
:li.0xCC: IPv4
:li.0xCD: ARP (ARPA)
:li.0xCE: FlexNet
:li.0xCF: NET/ROM
:li.0xF0: no layer-3 protocol; the payload is raw text
:li.0xFF: escape character for a two-byte protocol field
:eul.
:eul.
:p.:hp1.The :q.no layer-3:eq. AX.25 operating mode is used for
connections from a packet terminal to a BBS or packet radio
command-line shell node.:ehp1.
:p.In an unconnected state, you can send and receive UI-frames by using
some specific command that the TNC or packet stack provides; there is
no acknowlegement. In a connected state, I-frames are sent instead and
both sides expect to exchange those RR frames. The :hp5.window
size:ehp5. controls how many packets each side can exchange before a RR
frame must be sent. Finding a good window size is crucial to reliable
network links! As mentioned above, the sequence numbers run :hp2.0 to
7:ehp2., and so does the range for possible window size values.
:lblbox.A Bigger Window?
:p.There is a mechanism to get more than 8 packets in a window, and it
is called :hp3.modulo-128:ehp3.; it lengthens the size of the control
field to three bytes (with byte 1 being the frame type, byte 2 being
the modulo-8 window size, and byte 2 and 3 together being the
modulo-128 window size). Most packet radio stacks support this, but no
real TNCs do as far as I am aware.
:elblbox.
:i2.Window Size
:h5.From Packet to Transmission
:p.The next step of transmission occurs if you are driving your packet
TNC from a PC running the AX.25 network stack code on a PC.
:fn.Most TNCs from the 1980s and 1990s have a ROM that provides a
simple command-line interface to AX.25 no-layer-3 connections (that is,
simple textual connections) without the aid of a PC.
:efn.
Before being sent to the TNC, the packet needs to be wrapped in a
:hp3.KISS:ehp3. frame. KISS, short for Keep It Simple Stupid, is a
protocol that is very reminiscent of the UNIX SLIP (Serial Line IP)
protocol. The AX.25 frame is wrapped in :pk.0xC0:epk. bytes, and any of
those bytes encountered in the packet are escaped with a double-byte
escape. Examine the following diagram of a KISS frame:
:i2.KISS
:fig width=column place=inline frame=none.
:artwork name=prkiss align=center.
:figcap.KISS + AX.25 frame structure
:efig.
:p.Now, just before transmission (this is done in the TNC), the AX.25
frames are stripped of their KISS framing and wrapped into an
:hp3.HDLC:ehp3. frame, just like X.25. HDLC (High-level Data Link
Control) is the equivalent of the Ethernet MAC protocol for X.25
networks -- it provides a very simple link layer protocol that X.25
rode on top of. AX.25 is similar, except it replaces most of the fields
of an HDLC frame; AX.25 encapsulated into HDLC only adds the frame mark
byte (which is always :pk.0x7E:epk.) and a frame check sequence (the
standard HDLC CRC algorithm computes this) at the end.
:i2.HDLC
:p.Examine the following diagram to see an HDLC-encapsulated AX.25
frame:
:fig width=column place=inline frame=none.
:artwork name=prhdlc align=center.
:figcap.HDLC + AX.25 frame structure
:efig.
:lblbox.Two link-layer protocols?
:p.This is a common question heard when discussing non-TCP/IP
protocols; a number of non-Ethernet-based protocols have what one might
would describe as more than one link-layer protocol! X.25 is a
network-layer protocol, and it used HDLC over :hp1.synchronous-serial
links:ehp1. (similar to KISS). Now, when the AX.25 frame is sent to the
TNC, it is wrapped in a KISS encapsulation; when that packet arrives at
the TNC from the host computer, the TNC's firmware will strip the KISS
encapsulation off, re-frame it into HDLC :hp1.instead:ehp1. instead of
KISS, then proceed with the packet encoding and modulation.
:elblbox.
:p.Once the packet has arrived at the TNC and has been re-framed in
HDLC framing, it will be encoded using some kind of serial data
encoding algorithm (like NRZ, NRZI, or Manchester coding), modulated,
and the audio stream will be sent to the radio for transmission.
:p.For the TNCs that use 6PACK framing, the TNC directly speaks an
HDLC-like protocol (and the attached computer is much more aware of the
status of the RF channel). The 6PACK protocol results in the TNC
telling the host computer whenever it is receiving data, and the host
computer can trigger (in much more detail) partial transmissions.
:lblbox.What's the point of 6PACK?
:p.6PACK is an alternate framing method (i.e. :q.line discipline:eq.)
to KISS that allows for multi-dropped TNCs (multiple TNCs on a serial
port daisy chain). TNCs that provide this function are rare in the
modern era, but it was once provided by swapping out the ROM chips in
the TNC.
:elblbox.
:h3.NET/ROM
:p.When AX.25's limit of two digipeater hops started to become an issue
in the late 80s (as packet networks were exploding in popularity by
this time), a solution needed to be found, and found fast! This
originally came in the form of a ROM chip (made by Software 2000) that
replaced the factory ROM in a TNC-2 compatible TNC (such as the
ubiquitous ones made by MFJ), and was called :q.the Net ROM:eq.. Soon
thereafter, the protocol it spoke became known as :hp2.NET/ROM.:ehp2.
:i1.NET/ROM
:p.NET/ROM alleviates the two-hop constraint by allowing nodes to set
up virtual circuits between themselves, then packets could pass through
many nodes simply by passing through those virtual circuit tunnels.
:hp5.Since AX.25 has a limitation of one virtual circuit tunnel per
callsign pair per protocol number, it is highly recommended that you
use a different SSID for your :q.basic AX.25 connections:eq. and your
NET/ROM node.:ehp5.
:fn.In an ideal world, you could use the same callsign for both base
AX.25 (i.e. no-L3 connections to a node or BBS) and NET/ROM, but due to
a practical limit in the old NET/ROM TNC firmware of the past (and
apparently in some modern packet radio stacks), you must use different
SSIDs -- this is because the node lumps all packets from one node to
another under the same umbrella, regardless of the protocol ID number.
:efn.
:p.Once the NET/ROM tunnels have been established, the same frame types
are used as AX.25 used; to start a connection, :pk.SABM:epk.
and:pk.UA:epk. frames are used, then I-frames and :pk.RR:epk. frames
throughout the duration of the session!
:h5.Addressing Scheme
:p.Rather than relying on callsigns explicitly, NET/ROM often uses
:hp1.node name aliases.:ehp1. For instance, your BBS could be
named:pk.NYBBS:epk., and your own node could be named :pk.WECNOD:epk..
In order to build up a collection of nodes and adjacency, NET/ROM nodes
emit :hp5.node broadcasts:ehp5. on a fixed timer (which is inevitably
changeable by the user); once adjacent nodes receive these packets, the
virtual circuit tunnels open and NET/ROM packets may pass.
:i2.Addressing
:p.Some packet radio stacks (namely XRouter) refer to a NET/ROM
connection as a :q.layer 4:eq. connection; by a contrast, a no-L3
connection would be a :q.layer 2:eq. connection.
:fn.Remember, a no-L2 connection is a plain AX.25 textual connection!
:efn.
:lblbox.So, what are all these layers?
:p.Network stacks are divvied up into :q.layers:eq., and this notation
is used to conceptualize network protocol families. To provide you with
a visual overview, please see the below image.
:elblbox.
:fig width=column place=inline frame=none.
:artwork name=prlayers align=center.
:figcap.Packet radio protocol layers
:efig.
:h5.Routing and Quality
:p.NET/ROM, as mentioned before, uses node names to build up its
routing table. As more and more NET/ROM nodes pop up on a network and
begin to exchange routing tables, there needs to be some kind of way of
preventing a long path from being taken when a shorter path is
possible. This is remedied by the :hp5.quality metric:ehp5. associated
with the port.
:i2.Routing (quality)
:p.NET/ROM routing broadcasts emitted from a port will contain a
quality, and any subsequent retransmissions of other routing tables
will have some quality shaved off with each routing table emission from
each node along a path.
:h5.NET/ROM Serial Protocol
:p.When the original NET/ROM-capable TNC ROMs became available in the
mid-80s, KISS mode was the :hp1.de facto:ehp1. standard for driving
TNCs; for efficiency reasons, several hams realized they could drop the
AX.25 encapsulation and :hp1.just:ehp1. carry NET/ROM packets to and
from the TNC. This became known as the :hp2.NRS:ehp2. protocol.
:i2.NRS (NET/ROM Serial Protocol)
:h3.ROSE
:p.The RATS Open Systems Environment is a direct port of the :hp1.X.25
PLP:ehp1. (Packet Layer Protocol) to run on top of AX.25. Like the
original X.25 PLP, ROSE uses the standard 10-digit X.25 addressing
scheme (frequently called :hp3.X.121:ehp3. addresses) and requires
manual mapping of callsigns to network numbers. Like NET/ROM, full
routing is permitted.
:i1.ROSE
:p.Unfortunately, ROSE was not very well-adopted; the Linux kernel's
AX.25 stack contains the most complete implementation of ROSE, but even
it is buggy and not worth running.
:h3.Internet Protocol
:p.Despite many efforts to establish some alternate protocol as the
dominant network protocol of the world, the :hp1.ARPA Internet
Protocol:ehp1. became the world standard; you probably used IP to get
this document!
:i1.IP (Internet Protocol)
:p.IP is very often seen alongside several other protocols riding on
top of it:
:ul.
:li.:pk.TCP:epk. (Transmission Control Protocol), used to set up
virtual circuits with reliable connections that handle dropouts,
unexpected disconnections, and other similar failures
:li.:pk.UDP:epk. (User Datagram Protocol), used to transmit
non-reliably-delivered datagrams between hosts
:li.:pk.ICMP:epk. (Internet Control Message Protocol), used to allow
network hosts to test communication (this is the :pk.ping:epk.
command), detect adjacent routers (ICMP redirects), and alert hosts
that UDP ports are not listening
:li.:pk.SCTP:epk. (Stream Control Transmission Protocol), an evolved
version of TCP that never caught on
:eul.
:p.The structure of an IP packet is as follows:
:fig width=column place=inline frame=none.
:artwork name=prippkt align=center.
:figcap.IPv4 and IPv6 Packets
:efig.
:p.The source and destination IP addresses can be seen, as well as the
:hp2.protocol:ehp2. number, which will be discussed further down. The
:hp2.TTL:ehp2. (Time-to-Live) field specifies the amount of routing
hops a packet can take before routers will stop routing it.
:h5.IPv4 and IPv6
:p.Furthermore, there are two distinct versions of IP: :hp3.IPv4:ehp3.
and :hp3.IPv6:ehp3.: IPv6 was created to rectify the
address-space-exhaustion issue IPv4 was facing in the late 90s. IPv4
addressing takes the form of four dotted-decimal :hp5.octets:ehp5.
(i.e. an eight-bit number; these run 0 to 255) -- for
example, :pk.44.192.15.221:epk.. IPv6 addresses, on the other hand, are
much more complex (as they consist of eight hexadecimal 16-bit words,
running :pv.0000:epv. to :pv.FFFF:epv.):
:xmp.
2062&colon.41FE&colon.653A&colon.9882&colon.511&colon.FFE9&colon.8392&colon.412D
:exmp.
Note that zeros are omitted from this IPv6 addressing scheme -- an
address that has entire words of zeros can be abbreviated.
Take the following example IPv6 address:
:xmp.
2062&colon.41FE&colon.653A&colon.0000&colon.0000&colon.0000&colon.8392&colon.412D
:exmp.
This can be shortened down to:
:xmp.
2062&colon.41FE&colon.653A&colon.&colon.8392&colon.412D
:exmp.
:h5.Subnetting
:p.Another important concept in IP networking are the important
definitions of the :hp1.host address, host address, broadcast address.,
and netmask.:ehp1. The host address is what you already saw before --
something like :pk.192.168.9.21:epk.. For our example, let us say that
the netmask is set to :pk.255.255.255.0:epk.. Now, if you convert both
the network address and netmask into binary, you can discover what the
netmask does, what our network address is, and also our broadcast
address. Examine this example to see that conversion:
:i2.Subnetting
:xmp.
192.168.9.21     11000000.10101000.00001001.00010101
255.255.255.0    11111111.11111111.11111111.00000000
:exmp.
:p.When you examine this example, you can see that the subnet mask is
completely overlaying all of the first three octets of that host
address. If we zero out all the bits on the portion of the host address
that aligns with the zeros in the netmask, you get the network address;
likewise, if we set all of those bits to one, you get the broadcast
address :hp1.for that network.:ehp1. These addresses look like this:
:xmp.
192.168.9.21     11000000.10101000.00001001.00010101
255.255.255.0    11111111.11111111.11111111.00000000
192.168.9.0      11000000.10101000.00001001.00010000
192.168.9.255    11000000.10101000.00001001.11111111
:exmp.
:p.Now, let's up the ant&ea.! How about if we use that same address,
but we change the address to :pk.255.255.255.128:epk.. We can now
recompute the addresses:
:xmp.
192.168.9.21     11000000.10101000.00001001.00010101
255.255.255.128  11111111.11111111.11111111.10000000
192.168.9.0      11000000.10101000.00001001.00010000
192.168.9.127    11000000.10101000.00001001.01111111
:exmp.
:p.Rather than writing out long netmasks, there is an abbreviation
(colloquially called CIDR, pronounced :q.cider:eq.) that allows you to
write the netmask by just noting the amount of bits in the netmask.
Since the bit consumption of the netmask always marches left-to-right,
with no zeros that have any ones to the right of the address (at least
until the end), one can write :q.192.168.1.15 with a netmask of
255.255.255.0:eq. as :q.192.168.1.15/24:eq.!
:i3.CIDR notation
:p.IPv6 addressing follows the same scheme, except the examples are
large enough to roll off the page!
:lblbox.About subnetting
:p.The practice described above, :hp2.subnetting:ehp2., is used to
divide networks into different segments. Otherwise, every host on the
Internet would be on the same logical network, and a single broadcast
packet would easily bring the worldwide network to it knees. The key
thing to know here is that :hp1.hosts who share different network
addresses are no longer on the same network, and you must use a
:hp3.router:ehp3. to allow those hosts to communicate:ehp1. A router
forwards packets between networks, facilitating the creation of the
Internet.
:elblbox.
:h5.Link-Layer Address Resolution
:p.A key part of both IPv4 and IPv6 is the concept of discovering what
Ethernet MAC address/AX.25 callsign/PPP peer maps to an IP address.
IPv4 does this with a protocol called :hp3.ARP:ehp3., the Address
Resolution Protocol. IPv6 does this with the :hp3.Neighbor
Discovery:ehp3. subset of :hp3.ICMPv6:ehp3..
:p.To understand ARP, examine this simple example of two hosts trying
to communicate:
:ul.
:li.PC 1 and PC 2 are on an Ethernet network. PC 1 has the IP address
192.168.1.5, and PC 2 has the IP address 192.168.1.10 -- both are on
the same subnet (as in, they fall within the same network addressing
range). PC 1 sends a :pk.ping:epk. packet to PC 2.
:li.PC 1 does not know PC 2's Ethernet MAC address, so it cannot send
IP packets encapsulated in Ethernet frames. To remedy this, the
following things occur:
:ol.
:li.PC 1 sends an ARP request for 192.168.1.10 to the Ethernet
broadcast address, :pk.FF.FF.FF.FF.FF.FF:epk.
:li.PC 2 responds to PC 1's request by directing an ARP reply directly
to PC 1's MAC address.
:li.Both PC 1 and PC 2 know each others' MAC addresses, so they are
free to exchange the ping packet.
:eol.
:eul.
:p.Substitute :q.Ethernet:eq. for :q.AX.25:eq. and the portion above
would still read true.
:h5.Address Classes
:p.There is an important consideration to be had here: it is possible
to have an :hp1.implicit netmask:ehp1. based on the range that the host
address falls into. This table shows the class, range, and implicit
netmask:
:table cols='* * *'.
:tcap.IPv4 Address Classes
:row.
:c.:hp2.Class:ehp2.
:c.:hp2.Range:ehp2.
:c.:hp2.Default Netmask:ehp2.
:row.
:c.A
:c.0.0.0.0 - 127.255.255.255
:c.255.0.0.0
:row.
:c.B
:c.128.0.0.0 - 191.255.255.255
:c.255.255.0.0
:row.
:c.C
:c.192.0.0.0 - 223.255.255.255
:c.255.255.255.0
:row.
:c.D
:c.224.0.0.0 - 239.255.255.255
:c.:hp1.none, used for multicast:ehp1.
:row.
:c.E
:c.240.0.0.0 - 255.255.255.255
:c.:hp1.none, experimental:ehp1.
:etable.
:p.Class D addresses are used for :hp2.multicast:ehp2., wherein one
host can send a packet to several other hosts. This requires the medium
to be able to handle broadcast packets (which Ethernet and Wi-Fi, the
two most common link layer protocols that carry IP, support; so does
AX.25) or a router capable of processing :pk.IGMP:epk. packets
(Internet Group Management Protocol, which sets up multicast groups).
Once a multicast group has been set up, any host can send packets
directed to the multicast group address, and every host within that
group will be able to receive those packets (so long as they are
listening on the :hp1.port:ehp1. desired).
:h5.TCP and UDP
:p.As mentioned before, :pk.TCP:epk. and :pk.UDP:epk. are the most
common protocols that run on top of IP (besides ICMP); these are the
protocols that applications actually use to transfer data between
hosts. Since it would not make sense to only allow one connection
between two hosts, TCP and UDP provide a :hp2.port multiplexing:ehp2.
method wherein program listen on various port numbers; the other side,
which wishes to connect to a host listening on some port, simply opens
a connection to that port! Some ports are reserved for specific
application protocols; here are some common ports:
:table cols='* * *'.
:tcap.Common TCP and UDP Ports
:row.
:c.:hp2.Protocol:ehp2.
:c.:hp2.Application:ehp2.
:c.:hp2.Port Number:ehp2.
:row.
:c.TCP
:c.FTP
:c.20, 21
:row.
:c.TCP
:c.SSH
:c.22
:row.
:c.TCP
:c.Telnet
:c.23
:row.
:c.TCP
:c.SMTP
:c.25
:row.
:c.TCP/UDP
:c.DNS
:c.53
:row.
:c.UDP
:c.DHCP/BOOTP
:c.67
:row.
:c.UDP
:c.TFTP
:c.69
:row.
:c.TCP
:c.HTTP
:c.80
:row.
:c.TCP
:c.POP3
:c.110
:row.
:c.UDP
:c.NTP
:c.123
:row.
:c.TCP
:c.IMAP4
:c.143
:row.
:c.UDP
:c.SNMP
:c.161
:row.
:c.TCP
:c.HTTPS
:c.443
:etable.
:p.TCP and UDP themselves are identified in the IP packet through the
:hp2.protocol number:ehp2. which follows the destination IP and source
IP; here are some common IP protocol numbers:
:table cols='* *'.
:tcap.Common IP Protocol Numbers
:row.
:c.:hp2.Protocol:ehp2.
:c.:hp2.Description:ehp2.
:row.
:c.1
:c.ICMP
:row.
:c.2
:c.IGMP
:row.
:c.4
:c.IP-in-IP
:row.
:c.6
:c.TCP
:row.
:c.17
:c.UDP
:row.
:c.50
:c.IPsec ESP
:row.
:c.51
:c.IPsec AH
:row.
:c.93
:c.AX.25-in-IP
:row.
:c.94
:c.KA9Q IP-in-IP
:etable.
:h5.Tunnels
:p.Since IPv4 is the dominant protocol on the internet, clever network
engineers have sought to run protocols that did not win the standards
race over the Internet -- this is accomplished in the form of a
:hp2.tunnel.:ehp2. For our purposes, we will be discussing :hp3.AXIP
tunnels:ehp3., since we wish to join otherwise-disconnected packet
radio networks over the internet -- examples for how to set this up on
the various packet radio stacks will be provided below (contingent on
support existing for them).
:h3.TexNet
:p.When you read the AX.25 Protocol Number list above, you probably saw
:hp1.TexNet:ehp1. on that list! This is a defunct protocol that is
similar to NET/ROM, and was used for the Texas/Oklahoma/Arkansas packet
network of the same name. Nearly all sites on TexNet were 9600 baud
stations, :hp1.in the mid-1980s!:ehp1. Needless to say, though it is
defunct, its position in the development of NET/ROM is important to at
least note.
:h1.The Linux Kernel AX.25 Stack
:h3.Compiling/Installing the Packages
:p.Before we get too carried away, you're going to want to be using a
Linux system that contains the following packages:
:i1.Linux AX.25
:i2.Compiling
:ul.
:li.:pk.libax25:epk., the base library for all Linux AX.25 programs
:li.:pk.ax25-apps:epk., a set of programs for making and receiving
AX.25 connections
:li.:pk.ax25-tools:epk., some programs used for configuring network
protocols and devices
:li.:pk.node:epk. or :pk.uronode:epk., to create and run a packet radio
node/shell
:fn.:q.node:eq. refers to the ax25-node package, not NodeJS! This
program is becoming increasingly hard to find, but can be found by
Googling :pk.node_0.3.2.tar.gz:epk..
:efn.
:li.:pk.aprsd:epk., for creating and running an APRS digipeater
:li.:pk.direwolf:epk., a software TNC, :hp1.install this if you do not
have a real TNC or do not want to use it:ehp1.
:li.:pk.inetutils:epk., which contains FTP/Telnet/ntalk servers and
clients
:li.:pk.socat:epk., used to create loopback TTY devices (fake serial
ports) -- this will be used for AX.25 tunnels
:eul.
:p.It is also recommended that you install these too for
troubleshooting and debugging:
:ul.
:li.:pk.net-tools:epk., which provides the
older :pk.ifconfig:epk., :pk.arp:epk. and :pk.route:epk. commands that
understand the various packet radio protocols
:li.:pk.tcpdump:epk., a terminal-based network traffic monitor tool
:li.:pk.wireshark:epk., a graphical network traffic monitor tool that
produces easy-to-follow lists of network traffic
:li.:pk.screen:epk. or :pk.tmux:epk., programs that allow you to run a
command-line program on a disconnectable virtual terminal
:eul.
:p.Some of this you'll probably need to compile yourself. Debian calls
the node program "ax25-node" to avoid interfering with the NodeJS
programming language runtime (see that footnote).
Do note that libax25 requires the Linux kernel headers; these can be
installed from most package repositories by installing a package called
linux-headers :hp1.(or something similar to that):ehp1.. Compiling
these is as easy as finding the source code, extracting the archive,
and compiling like so:
:xmp.
$ cd libax25
$ ./configure --prefix=/usr
$ make -j8
# make install          <--- note: requires root, prefix with sudo if necessary
$ cd ..
$ cd ax25-apps
$ ./configure --prefix=/usr
$ make -j8
# make install
$ cd ..
$ cd ax25-tools
$ ./configure --prefix=/usr
$ make -j8
# make install
$ cd ..
$ cd node               <--- note: UROnode compiles the same way
$ ./configure  --prefix=/usr
$ make -j8
# make install
$ cd ..
# ln -s /usr/etc/ax25 /etc/ax25
:exmp.
:p.:hp1.Note: these instructions are not entirely exact; if you are not
passingly familiar with UNIX software installation and maintenance, I
recommend you just use the packages available in your Linux
distribution's package repository!:ehp1.
:h3.Configuring the Base Stack
:p.Linux has two ways of identifying AX.25 ports:
:ih2.Configuring Ports
:ul.
:li.The :hp1.port name:ehp1., a textual name that the AX.25 commands use
:li.The :hp1.interface name:ehp1., the name that the Linux kernel knows
the network device by
:fn.These devices always start with :pk.ax:epk. so, :pk.ax0:epk..
:efn.
:eul.
:p.To define an AX.25 port, edit :pk./etc/ax25/axports:epk., and, use
tabs for everything, not spaces:
:i3.AX.25
:xmp.
# name callsign speed paclen window description
#----- -------- ----- ------ ------ -----------
radio  WA4XYZ-1 1200  256    7      Real TNC
:exmp.
:p.Each field is as follows:
:ul.
:li.:pk.name:epk.: a descriptive name of the port (the :q.port name:eq.
described above)
:li.:pk.callsign:epk.: the unique callsign and SSID used for the port
:fn.For a terminal callsign (as in, your own personal terminal), if you
wish to use it, simply replace the SSID with zero. For
example: :pk.WA4XYZ-0:epk..
:efn.
:li.:pk.speed:epk.: the baud rate used on the interface's serial link
:fn.This is the :hp3.serial port baud rate:ehp3., not the RF modem baud
rate -- configuring that on a real TNC is likely something you will
need to read further down to learn about.
:efn.
:li.:pk.paclen:epk.: the packet length, 256 is standard for VHF/UHF
links
:li.:pk.window:epk.: the window size for the port (see the above notes
in the AX.25 section)
:li.:pk.description:epk.: a bit of text for your convinence
:eul.
:p.For :hp3.NET/ROM:ehp3. ports, you want to
edit :pk./etc/ax25/nrports:epk. (again, use tabs instead of spaces):
:i3.NET/ROM
:xmp.
# name callsign alias  paclen description
#----- -------- ------ ------ -----------
netrom WA4XYZ-7 EVIE1  236    NET/ROM Switch Port
:exmp.
:p.Likewise, the fields are as follows:
:ul.
:li.:pk.name:epk.: the port name
:li.:pk.callsign:epk.: the unique NET/ROM callsign for this port
:fn.Remember the footnote earlier? It is highly recommended that you do
not, under any circumstances, reuse a callsign/SSID pair used anywhere
else! Doing so breaks AX.25-noL3 connections (and sometimes also
NET/ROM connections)!
:efn.
:li.:pk.alias:epk.: the NET/ROM node alias, with a maximum length of
:hp3.6:ehp3. letters
:li.:pk.paclen:epk.: the packet length of the NET/ROM frames emitted
from this interface. :hp1.It is important that it is :hp3.20 bytes
less:ehp3. than the paclen of the AX.25 interface that will do the most
NET/ROM networking!:ehp1.
:li.:pk.description:epk.: text
:eul.
:p.You also need to define the port to the NET/ROM daemon (used to send
and receive routing frames), so edit :pk./etc/ax25/nrbroadcast:epk.:
:xmp.
# ax25_name min_obs def_qual worst_qual verbose
#---------- ------- -------- ---------- -------
radio       5       192      100        0
:exmp.
:p.The fields are as follows:
:ul.
:li.:pk.ax25_name:epk.: the corresponding AX.25 port that you want to
send and receive NET/ROM node broadcasts on
:li.:pk.min_obs:epk.: the minimum obsolecence value for the port (the
time before nodes are removed from the table)
:li.:pk.def_qual:epk.: the default quality announced when the node
includes itself in a routing table frame broadcast; the 192 to 200
range is fine
:li.:pk.worst_qual:epk.: the lowest quality number this node will
accept for other nodes listed in routing table frames
:li.:pk.verbose:epk.: set this to :hp2.1:ehp2. to allow this node to
announce :hp1.other NET/ROM nodes that it has heard -- this allows it
to function as a NET/ROM router (which is probably what you
want).:ehp1.
:eul.
:p.For :hp3.ROSE:ehp3., edit :pk./etc/ax25/rsports:epk.:
:i3.ROSE
:xmp.
# name address     description
#----- ----------  -----------
rose   1000100002  ROSE Switch Port
:exmp.
:p.The fields are as follows:
:ul.
:li.:pk.name:epk.: the switch port name
:li.:pk.address:epk.: the 10-digit X.121 address for this node as it
applies to all TNC ports
:li.:pk.description:epk.: text
:eul.
:h5.Starting Ports and Daemons
:p.After you have defined all of those ports, you now need to start
them. In this day and age, most Linux distributions do not
automatically load the Linux kernel modules for packet radio, so load
them yourself:
:ih2.Port configuration/daemons
:i3.Modules
:fn.You don't need to do this if you compiled your own monolithic
kernel with the modules baked in.
:efn.
:xmp.
# modprobe ax25
# modprobe netrom
# modprobe rose
:exmp.
:p.Once that has been done, you must now associate your TNC serial port
with your interface name (note that you will not need to set the baud
rate ahead of time) with :pk.kissattach:epk.:
:i3.kissattach
:xmp.
# kissattach /dev/ttyS0 radio
:exmp.
:p.If you are using a 6PACK TNC in lieu of a KISS TNC, you can
use :pk.spattach:epk. or the :pk.-6:epk. option of :pk.kissattach:epk.:
:xmp.
# kissattach -6 /dev/ttyS0 radio
# spattach /dev/ttyS0 radio
:exmp.
:p.If your TNC requires CRC (Direwolf does, but that will be covered in
a future section), enable it with this command:
:i3.kissparms
:xmp.
# kissparms -c 1 -p radio
:exmp.
:p.You now need to initialize your NET/ROM and ROSE ports:
:i3.nrattach
:i3.rsattach
:xmp.
# nrattach netrom
# rsattach rose
:exmp.
:p.In order for NET/ROM to work, you must start its daemon:
:i3.netromd
:xmp.
# netromd -i -t 10
:exmp.
:p.This transmits a routing table frame as soon as the program starts,
and then every 10 minutes after that.
:p.In order to build up a list of heard AX.25 stations (which can be
accessed with the :pk.mheard:epk. command later), run this daemon:
:xmp.
# mheardd
:exmp.
:p.:hp1.IPv4 over AX.25 will be discussed later.:ehp1.
:h5.Making Connections
:p.By the time you have completed the above commands, you should have
several interfaces added to the output of the :pk.ifconfig:epk.
command; invoke it and see for yourself:
:i2.ifconfig output
:fn.It is highly recommended that you install the
older:pk.net-tools:epk. package to have the :pk.ifconfig:epk. command
(among others), as the newer :pk.iproute2:epk. package is not very good
with packet radio.
:efn.
:xmp.
ax0: flags=67<UP,BROADCAST,RUNNING>  mtu 256
        ax25 WA4XYZ-1  txqueuelen 10  (AMPR AX.25)
        RX packets 919130  bytes 52973823 (50.5 MiB)
        RX errors 0  dropped 0  overruns 0  frame 0
        TX packets 1791658  bytes 235437942 (224.5 MiB)
        TX errors 0  dropped 0 overruns 0  carrier 0  collisions 0

nr0: flags=193<UP,RUNNING,NOARP>  mtu 255
        netrom WA4XYZ-7  txqueuelen 1000  (AMPR NET/ROM)
        RX packets 0  bytes 0 (0.0 B)
        RX errors 0  dropped 0  overruns 0  frame 0
        TX packets 0  bytes 0 (0.0 B)
        TX errors 264196  dropped 0 overruns 0  carrier 0  collisions 0

rose0: flags=193<UP,RUNNING,NOARP>  mtu 128
        rose 1000100002  txqueuelen 1000  (AMPR ROSE)
        RX packets 0  bytes 0 (0.0 B)
        RX errors 0  dropped 0  overruns 0  frame 0
        TX packets 264196  bytes 8454272 (8.0 MiB)
        TX errors 0  dropped 0 overruns 0  carrier 0  collisions 0
:exmp.
:p.The callsigns can be seen on the ports, which is correct.
:p.Now, to open a connection to another node, do so with
the :pk.call:epk. command:
:ih2.Connection commands
:i3.AX.25
:xmp.
$ call radio n4abc-10
:exmp.
:p.This will start a fullscreen program that allows you to have a basic
textual conversation with another user's terminal, packet node, packet
BBS, or some other such thing -- it depends on how they have their node
configured and what it is listening for. To escape from a session,
enter a tilde, followed by a period, then strike Enter.
:fn.This is the same break key sequence that :pk.cu:epk.
and:pk.ssh:epk. use.
:efn.
:p.To make a NET/ROM connection, use a similar command:
:i3.NET/ROM
:xmp.
$ call netrom NYBBS
:exmp.
:p.To make a ROSE connection, use this:
:i3.ROSE
:xmp.
$ call rose AA4XY 1019542251
:exmp.
:h5.Listening and Beaconing
:p.Inevitably, you will want to discover adjacent NET/ROM nodes, listen
for AX.25 packets on the air, and send out beacons. To discover
adjacent NET/ROM nodes, run these two commands:
:ih2.Adjacent node discovery
:i3.NET/ROM nodes files
:xmp.
$ cat /proc/net/nr_nodes
$ cat /proc/net/nr_neigh
:exmp.
:p.If you want to listen for AX.25 packets on all interfaces, run this:
:i3.AX.25 listen command
:xmp.
# listen -c -a
:exmp.
:p.If you wish to view a list of stations you have heard so far, invoke
this (note: if you are tuned to the APRS frequency, you may get
hundreds of lines of output in a busy city):
:i3.AX.25 mheard command
:xmp.
$ mheard
:exmp.
:p.To send out a beacon announcing your presence, you can do so by
transmitting AX.25 UI-frames on a fixed timer (this transmits it on the
AX.25 port named :pk.radio:epk.):
:i3.AX.25 beacon command
:xmp.
$ beacon -t 10 radio "WA4XYZ Packet Radio Node"
:exmp.
:p.While connected, you can use :pk.netstat:epk. to get information
about active connections:
:i3.Multiprotocol netstat command
:xmp.
$ netstat --ax25
$ netstat --netrom
$ netstat --rose
:exmp.
:h3.Running TCP/IP on AX.25
:p.IP is heavily used on packet radio networks in places where advanced
packet adoption is high, so it is best to familiarize oneself with it.
Please note that the :hp1.IP addresses used here are non-global, not
unique on the internet, and you should probably go get some IP
addresses that start with 44 (which you can discover how to do after
some brief internet searches).:ehp1. Alas, let us construct a sample
example with LAN addresses.
:i2.TCP/IP
:p.Before going further, you should assign an IP address to your AX.25
interface:
:xmp.
# ifconfig ax0 192.168.44.100
:exmp.
:p.Note that :pk.ax0:epk. corresponds to the AX.25 port
named :pk.radio:epk., and you can make this association by observing the
output from the :pk.kissattach:epk. command.
:p.Rather than having to retroactively assign the IP address using
the :pk.ifconfig:epk. command, you can actually specify it as the last
argument to the :pk.kissattach:epk. command:
:xmp.
# kissattach /dev/ttyS0 radio 192.168.44.100
:exmp.
:p.At this point, you should be able to ping other hosts. It is
recommend that you send one ping at a time, though, as to not overwhelm
the frequency with packets every second:
:xmp.
$ ping -c 1 192.168.44.125
:exmp.
:p.When this ping command starts, it will emit ARP packets (just like
described in the IP intro section before); when the other node replies,
IP commuication can start.
:h5.Telnet Daemon
:p.Note that :hp3.you should not use encrypted protocols:ehp3., like
SSH, over AX.25; instead, use Telnet. To start a Telnet server and
permit remote login into your node (which is a :hp2.security
risk:ehp2.), continue reading.
:i3.Telnet
:p.To run a Telnet server on a Linux system with :pk.systemd:epk.
installed (which is most of them), install :pk.inetutils:epk. and run
this command:
:xmp.
# systemctl start telnet@.socket
:exmp.
:p.To run a Telnet server without the aid of systemd and instead use
the :pk.inetd:epk. program included in inetutils,
edit :pk./etc/inetd.conf:epk. and add in the following line:
:xmp.
telnet  stream  tcp   nowait  root  /usr/libexec/telnetd telnetd
telnet  stream  tcp6  nowait  root  /usr/libexec/telnetd telnetd
:exmp.
:p.At this point, invoke :pk.inetd:epk.:
:xmp.
# /usr/libexec/inetd
:exmp.
:p.:hp3.Note for Arch Linux users:ehp3.: if you are using Arch Linux
and running inetd to run a telnet server (in lieu of having systemd
control the socket), you need to replace
the :pk./usr/libexec/telnetd:epk. command invocation
in :pk.inetd.conf:epk. with the following:
:fn.For some reason, Arch Linux has a misplaced :pk.login:epk. program,
which is normally at :pk./sbin/login:epk.; this is a workaround to
allow telnetd to find the program.
:efn.
:xmp.
telnetd --exec-login=/usr/bin/login
:exmp.
:p.Alternatively, if you wish to use :pk.xinetd:epk. in lieu of
traditional inetd, edit :pk./etc/xinetd.conf:epk. and make sure this is
in here:
:fn.Note that :pk.xinetd:epk. also likes to check
the :pk./etc/xinetd.d/*:epk. files; this behavior can be altered by
editing :pk./etc/xinetd.conf:epk..
:efn.
:xmp.
service telnet {
    flags          = REUSE
    socket_type    = STREAM
    wait           = no
    user           = root
    server         = /usr/libexec/telnetd
    server_args    = --exec-login=/usr/bin/login
    log_on_failure += USERID
    disable        = no
}
:exmp.
:p.You can start xinetd by simply typing :pk.xinetd:epk. on the command
line. :hp1.Note that the Arch Linux fix has already been applied
here:ehp1..
:p.To make a Telnet connection, simply type something like this:
:xmp.
$ telnet 192.168.44.95
:exmp.
:p.Note that when the connection opens, you will see the remote
system's login prompt; you do not directly specify the username on the
command line.
:h5.Routing - Basic
:p.If you have two networks, say :pk.192.168.1.0/24:epk.
and:pk.192.168.44.0/24:epk. (the packet radio :q.LAN:eq. used in the
above example). If we wish to route between these two networks, two
things need to happen:
:i3.Routing
:ol.
:li.The routing node (our packet radio box) needs to have :hp2.IP
forwarding:ehp2. (i.e. routing) enabled
:li.All nodes on both networks need to be made aware that they can
route to the other network, and told how to get there
:eol.
:p.Examine the following diagram of the setup:
:fig width=column place=inline frame=none.
:artwork name=prrtng align=center.
:figcap.IP Routing Example
:efig.
:p.For PC 1 to reach PC 2, it must pass the packets through the routing
node. First, you must engage :hp2.IP forwarding:ehp2. (routing) on the
packet node:
:xmp.
# echo 1 > /proc/sys/net/ipv4/conf/all/forwarding
:exmp.
:p.Now, we have to populate routing tables. For this to be done, you
should first check the output from :pk.ifconfig:epk. on the packet node:
:xmp.
eth0: flags=4675<UP,BROADCAST,RUNNING,ALLMULTI,MULTICAST>  mtu 1500
        inet 192.168.1.5  netmask 255.255.255.0  broadcast 192.168.1.25
        ether aa:00:04:00:07:04  txqueuelen 1000  (Ethernet)
        RX packets 310597188  bytes 69593025608 (64.8 GiB)
        RX errors 0  dropped 4673197  overruns 0  frame 0
        TX packets 28622761  bytes 3296809890 (3.0 GiB)
        TX errors 0  dropped 0 overruns 0  carrier 0  collisions 0

ax0: flags=67<UP,BROADCAST,RUNNING>  mtu 256
        inet 192.168.44.100 netmask 255.255.255.0  broadcast 192.168.100.25
        ax25 WA4XYZ-1  txqueuelen 10  (AMPR AX.25)
        RX packets 919130  bytes 52973823 (50.5 MiB)
        RX errors 0  dropped 0  overruns 0  frame 0
        TX packets 1791658  bytes 235437942 (224.5 MiB)
        TX errors 0  dropped 0 overruns 0  carrier 0  collisions 0
:exmp.
:p.First, you need to tell :hp1.PC 1:ehp1. how to reach the packet
radio subnet (which, naturally, includes PC 2):
:xmp.
# route add -net 192.168.44.0/24 gw 192.168.1.5
:exmp.
:p.Remember, :pk.192.168.1.5:epk. is the Ethernet port on the packet
node!
:p.Likewise, you need to provide PC 2 with a routing table entry. This
consists of:
:xmp.
# route add -net 192.168.1.0/24 gw 192.168.44.100
:exmp.
:p.Now, you can execute ping commands (or anything else that uses
TCP/IP) and route between the two networks!
:h5.Routing - NAT
:p.If you wish to access the Internet via packet radio, you will need
to employ :hp2.NAT:ehp2. (Network Address Translation) routing.
:fn.:q.NAT:eq. used here is a colloquial term for what Cisco IOS calls
:hp2.NAT Overload:ehp2., some network engineers call :hp2.PAT:ehp2.
(Port Address Translation), and Windows calls :q.Internet Connection
Sharing.:eq. There is some debate as to which term is the true one, but
most people just call it :q.NAT.:eq.
:efn.
:p.Examine the following image to visualize the setup:
:fig width=column place=inline frame=none.
:artwork name=prnat align=center.
:figcap.IP NAT Example
:efig.
:p.Note that the IP addresses on the Ethernet subnet are no longer
shown -- they are no longer significant. Instead, what matters most is
the packet radio subnet. Now, enter the following commands on the
packet node to engage NAT routing, then I will explain what is going on
and why.
:xmp.
# iptables -t nat -a POSTROUTING -o eth0 -j MASQUERADE
# iptables -A FORWARD -i eth0 -o ax0 -m state --state RELATED,ESTABLISHED -j ACCEPT
# iptables -A FORWARD -i ax0 -o eth0 -j ACCEPT
:exmp.
:p.So, what's going on here? Well, whenever a host on the packet radio
subnet sends packets directed at the Internet, they will be
transmitting them to the packet routing node. It will then modify the
packet's source and destination address such that the packets look like
they are coming from the packet routing node PC; at the same time, the
router attached to the internet connection and Ethernet LAN does the
same thing.
:fn.This is a :q.double-NAT:eq. setup, since it does not require
entering any routes on that edge router.
:efn.
:h3.BPQ Ethernet Interface
:p.If you wish to run packet radio protocols in the gigabits range, the
BPQ Ethernet driver is your friend. The :pk.BPQETHER:epk. allows you to
encapsulate AX.25 frames (and, by extension, NET/ROM, ROSE, FlexNet,
IP, and such) over Ethernet (and, with some modification, Wi-Fi). This
provides a high-speed backbone and testing environment over Ethernet
LANs; non-Ethernet connections (T1 lines, synchronous serial links,
dialup links) will want to use an :hp1.AXIP or AXUDP:ehp1. tunnel
(which will be documented later).
:p.Before this can work, you must first :hp1.ensure that the Ethernet
device name:ehp1. has not been :q.renamed:eq. by something; in other
words, the network interface is named :pk.eth0:epk.. To do this, you
will need to pass a kernel parameter to the kernel; this can be done by
editing the GRUB/ELILO/Limine configuration. How to do this is
out-of-scope for this document, but you will need to pass the following
parameter: :pk.net.ifnames=0:epk.
:p.Once that change has been made, you will need to enter another port
into :pk./etc/ax25/axports:epk. like so:
:xmp.
# name callsign speed paclen window description
#----- -------- ----- ------ ------ -----------
radio  WA4XYZ-1 1200  256    7      Real TNC
bpq    WA4XYZ-5 19200 1024   7      BPQETHER Port
:exmp.
:p.There is no :pk.kissattach:epk. command to run here, as we can
:hp1.directly:ehp1. use the port after assigning the address to
the :pk.bpq0:epk. device:
:xmp.
# ifconfig bpq0 hw ax25 WA4XYZ-5 up
:exmp.
:p.When this has been done, the :pk.call:epk. command will
read:pk./etc/ax25/axports:epk. to resolve the port name to its
associated callsign -- after this is done, the connection opens.
:p.It is also possible to enable that port for NET/ROM, simply by
editing :pk./etc/ax25/nrbroadcast:epk.:
:xmp.
# ax25_name min_obs def_qual worst_qual verbose
#---------- ------- -------- ---------- -------
radio       5       192      100        0
bpq         5       192      100        0
:exmp.
:p.It is not necessary to update the ROSE configuration.
:h3.AXIP and AXUDP Tunnels
:p.Sometimes, it is necessary to bridge a packet radio network over the
Internet (or some other network link that speaks TCP/IP natively, and
does not natively support AX.25). In order to do this, one can
construct an :hp2.AX/IP:ehp2. tunnel -- this encapsulates the AX.25
frames into IPv4 packets. Likewise, to encapsulate TCP/IP into UDP
packets, one can create an :hp2.AX/UDP:ehp2. tunnel.
:fn.While AX/IP packets are technically smaller than AX/UDP packets, it
is generally not possible to :q.port-forward:eq. an AX/IP tunnel. This
resulted in the creation of :hp1.AX/UDP:ehp1., which can be
port-forwarded through a NAT router and firewall.
:efn.
:p.Before creating an AX/IP or AX/UDP tunnel, it is necessary to
understand the differences between AX/IP and AX/UDP in detail:
:ul.
:li.:hp3.AX/IP:ehp3.: uses IP protocol number 93
:li.:hp3.AX/UDP:ehp3.: uses UDP port 10093 :hp1.(or whichever you'd
like):ehp1.
:eul.
:p.The program that controls this tunnel is :pk.ax25ipd:epk., and it
has two operating modes (IP and UDP, as mentioned before). The
configuration file is located at :pk./etc/ax25/ax25ipd.conf:epk. --
start with a sample like this for :hp3.AX/IP:ehp3. mode:
:xmp.
# Socket type:
socket ip

# Use mode "tnc" or "digi"
mode tnc

# Beaconing
beacon after 600
btext YOUR BEACON TEXT HERE

# Use the PTY device created with socat
device /dev/ttyS30

# Line speed
speed 115200

# Log level, 0 to 4 (4 being the highest)
loglevel 0

# Broadcast ourselves?
broadcast QST NODES

# AX/IP connections go here
route NH6AB-2 12.34.56.78
:exmp.
:p.Likewise, for an :hp3.AX/UDP:ehp3. tunnel, start with this:
:xmp.
# Socket type:
socket udp

# Use mode "tnc" or "digi"
mode tnc

# Beaconing
beacon after 600
btext YOUR BEACON TEXT HERE

# Use the PTY device created with socat
device /dev/ttyS30

# Line speed
speed 115200

# Log level, 0 to 4 (4 being the highest)
loglevel 0

# Broadcast ourselves?
broadcast QST NODES

# AX/IP connections go here
route NH6AB-1 12.34.56.78 udp 10093
:exmp.
:p.Now, one may wish to emit broadcasts on a tunnel, and/or also
provide a default route on that interface. This can be done with
the :pk.b:epk. and :pk.d:epk. letters appended to the end of
the :pk.route:epk. lines. For example, to set a link as both
broadcast-capable (in other words, for to make NET/ROM work) and a
default route:
:xmp.
route NH6AB-1 12.34.56.78 udp 10093 b d
:exmp.
:p.As you might imagine, you must also enter this
into :pk./etc/ax25/axports:epk., like so:
:xmp.
# name callsign speed paclen window description
#----- -------- ----- ------ ------ -----------
radio  WA4XYZ-1 1200  256    7      Real TNC
bpq    WA4XYZ-5 19200 1024   7      BPQETHER Port
axip   WA4XYZ-3 19200 1024   7      AX/IP Port
:exmp.
:p.Likewise, edit :pk./etc/ax25/nrbroadcast:epk. to make the port
elegible for NET/ROM communication:
:xmp.
# ax25_name min_obs def_qual worst_qual verbose
#---------- ------- -------- ---------- -------
radio       5       192      100        0
bpq         5       192      100        0
axip        5       192      100        0
:exmp.
:p.Once you have defined the :pk.axports:epk. entry, you can now create
the :hp1.fake serial port:ehp1. used to allow :pk.ax25ipd:epk. to
communicate with the Linux kernel; the :pk.socat:epk. program does this:
:xmp.# socat -d -d PTY,link=/dev/ttyS30 PTY,link=/dev/ttyS31
:exmp.
:p.Now that :pk.socat:epk. is running, you can now start the daemon:
:xmp.# ax25ipd
:exmp.
:h3.Accepting Inbound Connections
:p.Inevitably, in the construction of a packet radio network, you will
find it necessary to allow some packet nodes to communicate with your
node. This can be in the form of two different methods:
:ol.
:li.:hp2.Keyboard-to-keyboard chat:ehp2., with inbound connections
answered by a program like :pk.ttylinkd.:epk.
:li.:hp2.A node:ehp2., a packet radio :q.shell:eq. that provides
BBS-esque functions
:eol.
:p.This guide will cover the setup of both listed options.
:h5.Configuring ax25d
:p.Before you can accept any inbound connections, you will need to
configure a program called :pk.ax25d.:epk.
:fn.This is from the :pk.ax25-apps:epk. package.
:efn.
Configuration is relatively simple, and is done by editing
the :pk./etc/ax25/ax25d.conf:epk. configuration file. If you are
familiar with configuring :pk.inetd:epk., :pk.ax25d:epk. is intended to
be somewhat analogous to that. Examine the following sample
configuration file:
:xmp.
&lbrk.WA4XYZ-1 VIA radio&rbrk.
NOCALL   * * * * * *  L
default  * * * * * *  - root  /usr/local/bin/node   node
#
&lbrk.WA4XYZ-5 VIA bpq&rbrk.
NOCALL   * * * * * *  L
default  * * * * * *  - root  /usr/local/bin/node   node
#
&lbrk.WA4XYZ-3 VIA axip&rbrk.
NOCALL   * * * * * *  L
default  * * * * * *  - root  /usr/local/bin/node   node
#
<netrom>
NOCALL   * * * * * *  L
default  * * * * * *  - root  /usr/local/bin/node   node
#
{* via rose}
NOCALL  * * * * * *  L
default * * * * * *  -  root  /usr/local/bin/node   node
:exmp.
:p.In the above example, the :pk.node:epk. program is being invoked --
this is an example of :hp1.case 2:ehp1. on the above list. Substituting
some other program name will result in that program being invoked.
:p.Note also that this program can accept AX.25, NET/ROM, and ROSE
connections.
:h5.Configuring the Node Program
:p.:pk.Node:epk. is a basic packet radio shell program, allowing
inbound users to run commands of their choosing. The base program is
not very feature-rich, so adding custom commands is highly recommended.
:p.The configuration file is located at :pk./etc/ax25/node.conf:epk. --
consult the following example:
:xmp keep=5.
# Idle timeout (seconds).
IdleTimeout     900

# Timeout when gatewaying (seconds).
ConnTimeout     14400

# Visible hostname. Will be shown at telnet login.
HostName        my.host.name

# Node ID.
NodeId          WA4XYZ-1

# ReConnect flag.
ReConnect       on

# "Local" network.
LocalNet        192.168.1.0/24

# Hidden ports.
#HiddenPorts    2

# Netrom port name. This port is used for outgoing netrom connects.
NrPort          netrom

# Logging level
LogLevel        3

# The escape character (CTRL-T)
# Use this to return to the node if a telnet connection or comma
d fails or hangs.
EscapeChar      &hat.T

# Resolve IP numbers to DNS addresses?
ResolveAddrs    on

# Node prompt.
NodePrompt      "WA4XYZ-1>"
:exmp.
:p.Before starting :pk.node:epk., you must also configure some account
permissions. To do that, edit :pk./etc/ax25/node.perms:epk.:
:xmp.
# user  type    port    passwd  perms
# Default permissions per connection type.
# 63 means they can connect out to any host through telnet.
*       ax25    *       *       63
*       netrom  *       *       63
*       local   *       *       63
*       ampr    *       *       63
*       inet    *       *       63
*       host    *       *       63
:exmp.

:lblbox.A Word on Node Security
:p.Giving :pk.inet:epk. users (i.e. users that connected to the node
through Telnet) access to telnet out to any other host on the Internet
is a risky move. A clever attacker (though unlikely) could use this as
a way to frame you for all manner of denial-of-service attacks!
:elblbox.
:p.Now that user permissions have been set, you now should edit the
MOTD (Message of The Day) file to configure the banner users will see
when connecting to the node. Edit :pk./etc/ax25/node.motd:epk.:
:xmp.
This is John Smith's Super Duper Cool Packet Radio Node!

Type ? for a list of commands. help <commandname> gives a
description of the named command.
:exmp.
:p.Assuming that you have configured the node correctly this far, the
only thing that is required to start the program is actually to
start :pk.ax25d:epk.:
:xmp.# ax25d
:exmp.
:p.After testing the node, you will likely want to define some custom
commands to make the node do something useful. The command syntax
consists of the following elements:
:ol.
:li.:pk.ExtCmd:epk.
:li.:hp1.<command name>:ehp1.
:li.:hp1.<command flags:ehp1.
:ul.
:li.1: run command through pipe
:li.2: reconnect prompt
:li.3: change user and then run through pipe
:eul.
:li.:hp1.<username that the program runs at, likely root>:ehp1.
:li.:hp1.<full path to Linux program invoked when the command is ran>:ehp1.
:li.:hp1.<name of the program, without the full path>:ehp1.
:li.:hp1.<command arguments>:ehp1.
:eol.
:p.For some cooked examples, see the below lines:
:xmp.
ExtCmd PS 1 nobody /bin/ps ps ax
ExtCmd PMS 3 root /usr/sbin/pms pms
:exmp.
:p.You can also :hp2.allow TCP Node connections:ehp2. by allowing users
to connect via the Telnet protocol. This is done by adding a line
to :pk./etc/inetd.conf:epk. just like before for the standard Telnet
daemon:
:xmp.
node    stream  tcp   nowait  root  /usr/bin/node node
node    stream  tcp6  nowait  root  /usr/bin/node node
:exmp.
:p.However, before restarting :pk.inetd:epk., you must also
update :pk./etc/services:epk.:
:fn.:pk.inetd:epk. uses port names resolved from the lines
of :pk./etc/services:epk. by convention, but you can also replace
:q.node:eq. in the first word of the line with a port number to achieve
the same effect.
:efn.
:xmp.node    3694/tcp
:exmp.
:p.If you are using :pk.xinetd:epk. instead, the configuration is
similar :hp1.(but does still require the :pk./etc/services:epk. fix):ehp1.
:xmp.
service node {
    flags          = REUSE
    socket_type    = STREAM
    wait           = no
    user           = root
    server         = /usr/bin/node
    log_on_failure += USERID
    disable        = no
}
:exmp.
:p.If you are using :pk.systemd:epk., you will need to edit two files:
:ol.
:li.:pk./usr/lib/systemd/system/node.socket:epk.
:xmp.
&lbrk.Unit&rbrk.
Description=Packet Radio Node

&lbrk.Socket&rbrk.
ListenStream=3694
Accept=true

&lbrk.Install&rbrk.
WantedBy=sockets.target
:exmp.
:li.:pk./usr/lib/systemd/system/node@.service:epk.
:xmp.
&lbrk.Unit&rbrk.
Description=Node Server
After=local-fs.target

&lbrk.Service&rbrk.
ExecStart=-/usr/sbin/uronode
StandardInput=socket
:exmp.
:eol.
:p.To apply these changes, invoke the following two commands:
:xmp.# systemctl daemon-reload
# systemctl start node.socket
:exmp.
:h5.Using UROnode Instead of Node
:p.For the most part, it is highly recommended that you use
:hp3.UROnode:ehp3. in lieu of traditional Node; not only does UROnode
have more functionality, but it is more secure.
:p.Compilation and installation is relatively simple; at the time of
this document's composition, UROnode can be found on GitHub after a
quick Internet search -- if this information no longer applies in the
future, it should surely still be findable!
:p.However, there are some things that should be noted.
By default, the October 2021 version of UROnode deposits all files into
the :pk./usr/local:epk. prefix -- you can fix this by running the
following commands to compile UROnode:
:xmp.
$ export ETC_DIR=/etc
$ export SBIN_DIR=/usr/sbin
$ export BIN_DIR=/usr/bin
$ export LIB_DIR=/usr/lib
$ export DATA_DIR=/usr/share
$ export MAN_DIR=/usr/share/man
$ export VAR_DIR=/var/lib/ax25
$ ./configure
<say no to interactive mode>
$ make -j4
# make install
:exmp.
:p.Now that UROnode has been installed, it must now be configured.
Since UROnode is a fork of Classic Node,
editing :pk./etc/ax25/uronode.conf:epk. is :hp1.relatively:ehp1. simple!
Note that you must also edit these files; check them off as you go:
:ol prefix=checkoff.
:li.:pk./etc/ax25/uronode.announce:epk. - announcements and notices
:li.:pk./etc/ax25/uronode.info:epk. - information file for users that
run the :pk.info:epk. command
:li.:pk./etc/ax25/uronode.motd:epk. - Message of the Day
:li.:pk./etc/ax25/uronode.perms:epk. - user permissions, :hp1.it is
critical you understand the security ramnifications here!:ehp1. :
:li.:pk./etc/ax25/uronode.routes:epk. - static pinned routes such that
users do not have to specify a port name on outbound connections :
:li.:pk./etc/ax25/uronode.users:epk. - callsigns, usernames, and
passwords to allow sysop users to drop into a Linux shell
:eol.
:p.Note that you can also :hp1.edit inetd, xinetd, or systemd's
configuration:ehp1. to allow :hp3.TCP connections:ehp3. to UROnode;
simply replace :pk.node:epk. with :pk.uronode:epk. and it should work!
:h3.APRS Digipeater/I-Gate
:p.If you wish to build an APRS digipeater or I-Gate   with the Linux
AX.25 stack, you have several options:
:ol.
:li.:pk.aprx:epk., a dedicated APRS daemon
:fn.:pk.aprx:epk. was forked from the earlier :pk.aprsd:epk. project.
:efn.
:li.:hp2.NOSaprs:ehp2., see the JNOS section below
:li.:hp2.XRouter APRS:ehp2., see the XRouter section below
:li.:hp2.Dire Wolf:ehp2. integrated APRS support, see the Dire Wolf
section below
:eol.
:p.Since this manual portion is present amongst the Linux AX.25 setup,
let us examine :pk.aprx:epk.!
:h5.Compilation
:p.Compiling :pk.aprx:epk. is relatively simple:
:xmp.
sh configure
make -j4
sudo mkdir /usr/local/aprx
sudo cp aprx /usr/local/aprx
sudo cp aprx.8 /usr/local/aprx
:exmp.
:h5.Bidirectional I-Gate Configuration
:p.APRS I-Gates that can transmit are a :hp1.rather rare
commodity:ehp1. in the world of amateur radio. As such, you should
probaby try to build one! Start
editing :pk./usr/local/aprx/aprx.conf:epk.:
:xmp keep=5.
mycall N0CALL-10
myloc lat 3639.50N lon 08724.50W

<aprsis>
    passcode 12345
    server noam.aprs2.net
    filter "m/25"
</aprsis>

<logging>
    pidfile /usr/local/aprx/aprx.pid
    rflog /usr/local/aprx/aprx-rf.log
    aprxlog /usr/local/aprx/aprx.log
    #dprslog /usr/local/aprx/dprs.log
    #erlangfile /usr/local/aprx/aprx.state
</logging>

<interface>
   ax25-device        $mycall
   tx-ok              true
   telem-to-is        true
</interface>

<beacon>
    beaconmode both
    cycle-size 20m
    beacon object "N0CALL-10" symbol "I#" lat "1234.50N" lon "01234.50W"
         comment "N0CALL TXing I-Gate and Digi"
    beacon raw ")123456zThis is aprx 2.9.1"
</beacon>

<telemetry>
    transmitter       $mycall
    via               TRACE1-1
    source            $mycall
</telemetry>

<digipeater>
    transmitter       $mycall

    <source>
       source         $mycall
       relay-type     digipeated
       viscous-delay  0
       ratelimit      60 120
       filter         t/poimqstunw
    </source>

    <source>
       source         APRSIS
       relay-type     third-party
       viscous-delay  0
       ratelimit      60 120
       via-path       WIDE1-1
       msg-path       WIDE1-1
       filter         t/poimqstunw
    </source>
</digipeater>
:exmp.
:h3.Configuring Dire Wolf
:p.If you don't have a real TNC (or don't want to use it), Dire Wolf is
a software TNC that works marvels; WB2OSZ did a wonderful job writing
it!
:i1.Dire Wolf
:h5.Compiling and Installation
:p.To install this wonderful program, follow the compilation directions
on the GitHub page. I :hp2.highly:ehp2. recommend that you compile it
yourself, to ensure that you are getting the latest version!
:p.Of interest is the support for :hp1.CM108 USB sound card
keying:ehp1. through its GPIO pins -- the CM108 is a cheap USB sound
card famously known for the availability of some extra GPIO pins on its
controller, and these can be used to key the radio.
:h5.Configuration
:p.Here is a basic config file for your viewing pleasure, usually
located at :pk./usr/local/share/doc/direwolf/conf/direwolf.conf:epk.
(unless you did not install Dire Wolf from the source code). Ensure the
configuration file contains the following elements:
:xmp.
ADEVICE soundhw:4.0
CHANNEL 0
MYCALL WA4XYZ-1
MODEM 1200
ARATE 48000
DTMF
TXDELAY 5
TXTAIL 5
FULLDUP OFF
PTT /dev/ttyUSB0 RTS
AGWPORT 8000
KISSPORT 8001
:exmp.
:p.Breaking down the config file, the configuration statements are as
follows:
:ul.
:li.:pk.ADEVICE:epk. - select audio device
:fn.Use :pk.arecord -l:epk. to list audio devices.
:efn.
:li.:pk.CHANNEL:epk. - select channel 0 (Dire Wolf can emulate a
multi-port TNC)
:li.:pk.MYCALL:epk. - set default callsign, overwritten by the KISS
callsign
:li.:pk.MODEM:epk. - set modem RF baud rate
:li.:pk.ARATE:epk. - set sound card sample rate
:fn.48000 is good for 1200 and 2400; use 96000 for 9600 and 19200 baud
:efn.
:li.:pk.DTMF:epk. - do DTMF decoding
:li.:pk.TXDELAY:epk. - transmit keyup delay (time from PTT activation
to stable, modulated RF emission)
:li.:pk.TXTAIL:epk. - transmit tail
:li.:pk.FULLDUP:epk. - select full-duplex operating mode
:li.:pk.PTT:epk. - PTT device selection:
:ul.
:li.:pk.<serial port device name> <signal pin>:epk.: use the selected
TTY device and RTS/DTR for keying
:li.:pk.GPIO <pin number>:epk.: use Raspberry Pi GPIO pins for keying
(prefix the number with a - for an active-low signal
:li.:pk.CM108:epk.: use GPIO pin 3 of the CM108 or CM119 USB sound chip
:eul.
:li.:pk.AGWPORT:epk. - AGW Packet Engine socket
:li.:pk.KISSPORT:epk. - KISS socket
:eul.
:p.After configuring Dire Wolf, start it with this command:
:xmp.# direwolf -p
:exmp.
:p.This will cause Dire Wolf to start, and it will create a KISS TNC
PTY device at :pk./tmp/kisstnc:epk. You can then run :pk.kissattach:epk.
on that port, provided that you enter the port
into :pk./etc/ax25/axports:epk. as follows:
:xmp.
# name callsign speed paclen window description
#----- -------- ----- ------ ------ -----------
radio  WA4XYZ-1 1200  256    7      Real TNC
radio2 WA4XYZ-3 1200  256    7      Dire Wolf
:exmp.
:p.Don't forget to enter it into :pk./etc/ax25/nrports:epk. and start
the rest of the packet stack.
:p.:hp2.Important!:ehp2. You need to run :pk.kissparms -c 1 -p <Dire
Wolf port name>:epk. :hp1.before:ehp1. trying to make any connections
with any of the user programs!
:h3.List of Linux Packet Programs
:p.The following table provides a list of programs, their associated
package, and a brief description:
:table cols='* * *'.
:tcap.Linux Packet Programs
